Hi, Zack! It's been a long time since we had a
contributor here who was playing NetHack by feel.

 > PS: If I had a scroll of stair location or
 > somesuch, I'd use it!

Orbs will do that for you, but orbs are heavy,
scarce, and usually have only a few charges.

If you use the common strategy for exploring mazes:
"put your left hand on a wall and walk all the way
around the maze until you get back to your starting
point", you will sense most of the dungeon level,
usually 85% or more, except for some stuff in the
middle of open areas that isn't near a wall.

Then you need to go find those by feel, and "mow the
lawn" back and forth across them until you're sure
you haven't missed anything important, like the
stairs or the armor of your dreams.

You might have to start by walking one direction
until you are fairly convinced you are at the edge
of the dungeon level, so you aren't circling an
internal wall.

One problem that frequently catches new players is
when some object is sitting on the stairs, and
therefor hiding them. Unless you have already found
the stairs, you need to stand on each object, do a
"near-look" with the ":" command, and check for a
"you see here a staircase" message (or a spelling
close to that).

In your circuit of the dungeon level, if it is a
dark level, you will profit from the "step, search,
step, search" tactic, both because it gives you some
small improved chance of finding traps, and because
it easily shows you which way the wall is or is not
going next.

You'll want to check the side of your path which is
opposite from the wall, as you go, to see if objects
have been seen, too, at each step.

I hope that helps.

